1
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:06,220
Hello.

2
00:00:06,354 --> 00:00:10,460
This time, we'll look at setting up your working environment.

3
00:00:10,464 --> 00:00:13,820
First, I only use Photoshop in my class.

4
00:00:13,821 --> 00:00:20,940
There are lots of drawing programs, but the main reason I use Photoshop is that it's versatile and especially well-suited for coloring.

5
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:23,020
Its correction features are great too.

6
00:00:23,820 --> 00:00:33,900
When you buy Photoshop, Adobe offers several plans, and there's one called Photoshop Single App that I think a lot of people buy.

7
00:00:34,765 --> 00:00:37,720
I got tricked into buying that at first too.

8
00:00:37,721 --> 00:00:47,000
The apps included differ by plan, but since we only need Photoshop, you should just buy the Photography plan, which is about eleven thousand won a month.

9
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:50,360
I'm using version Creative Cloud 2022.

10
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:57,320
With a version that's too old, I can't properly use the brushes I use, so I'd recommend updating.

11
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,200
For the tool, I use a tablet.

12
00:01:00,201 --> 00:01:02,520
Up until two years ago I used an Intuos 3.

13
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:07,280
I bought it when I first started drawing and used it for about ten years.

14
00:01:07,281 --> 00:01:14,480
I thought about replacing it if it broke, but I used it as a cutting board and handled it roughly, and it was so sturdy it never broke.

15
00:01:14,481 --> 00:01:15,481
It wasn't a vertical one, by the way.

16
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:18,160
Now I use an Intuos Pro.

17
00:01:18,694 --> 00:01:22,560
The pressure levels are higher, so I can make more precise pressure adjustments.

18
00:01:22,561 --> 00:01:28,800
These days I draw with a relaxed hand, so the Intuos Pro, which has higher pressure levels, suits me well.

19
00:01:29,654 --> 00:01:37,380
If you end up getting hired by a company, they almost always issue you a Wacom tablet, so it's good to get used to one, and I'd recommend it.

20
00:01:38,259 --> 00:01:42,180
For monitors, I use the Dell UltraSharp series.

21
00:01:42,419 --> 00:01:44,420
It's a dual-monitor setup.

22
00:01:44,421 --> 00:01:48,980
If you're doing digital drawing, having at least dual monitors is essential.

23
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:55,860
For the sub monitor, if you rotate it vertically using the pivot feature, it helps when drawing tall concept art.

24
00:01:59,924 --> 00:02:02,830
Here's my Photoshop screen layout.

25
00:02:02,831 --> 00:02:04,670
I use the Korean version.

26
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:08,669
The navigator isn't strictly necessary, but I habitually keep it on.

27
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:12,311
I also habitually zoom in and out by clicking with the mouse.

28
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,710
The feature I think is important is this color picker, which uses HSB sliders.

29
00:02:18,224 --> 00:02:22,190
From top to bottom, you can adjust hue, saturation, and value.

30
00:02:22,484 --> 00:02:26,990
This color picker is effective for the relative use of color I'll explain later.

31
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:35,630
Then there's the layers panel. Since the navigator and color picker don't take up much space, I make the layers panel the biggest.

32
00:02:37,015 --> 00:02:40,000
Next is the History panel.

33
00:02:40,001 --> 00:02:47,200
It's good for comparing a picture before and after, and it lets you go back in time without pressing Control and Z many times.

34
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:57,280
If you check the box at the front, your work history is saved, and when you use the History brush, it recalls the saved state over the area you brush.

35
00:02:57,330 --> 00:03:00,400
This is something I use often, so do get familiar with it.

36
00:03:00,401 --> 00:03:02,320
Then there's the brushes panel.

37
00:03:02,321 --> 00:03:06,000
I've set mine so it just shows the brush stroke shapes.

38
00:03:06,344 --> 00:03:08,500
The reason is that it lets me see all the brushes at a glance.

39
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:12,061
You can configure it whichever way is comfortable for you.

40
00:03:13,335 --> 00:03:21,120
If you press Arrange, or use the New Window key, you can duplicate the picture you're working on and display it.

41
00:03:21,121 --> 00:03:27,801
I resize the duplicated window so I can see the whole picture, and put it on my second monitor.

42
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:36,161
And if you press the Flip Horizontal button, you can mirror just that window horizontally.

43
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:42,851
If you test it, you can see it updates in real time.

44
00:03:44,495 --> 00:03:49,841
Since it's important to see the whole picture, I always keep this up while I work.

45
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:56,920
Now let's look at keyboard shortcuts.

46
00:03:56,921 --> 00:04:00,680
These are shortcuts you probably already know, but let me quickly go through them.

47
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,000
First, B is the brush tool.

48
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,680
E is the eraser tool.

49
00:04:07,855 --> 00:04:12,760
The left bracket key reduces the size of the brush or eraser.

50
00:04:13,935 --> 00:04:17,720
The right bracket key increases it, and vice versa.

51
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:24,881
For the eyedropper, with the brush selected, hold Alt and click on a color area.

52
00:04:25,685 --> 00:04:28,871
That way you can also pick a color from anywhere on the canvas.

53
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:30,670
Next is the Lasso tool.

54
00:04:32,245 --> 00:04:35,630
It's also called the lasso tool, and you use it with the L key.

55
00:04:37,175 --> 00:04:43,680
If you hold Shift, it adds to your selection; if you hold Alt, it subtracts instead.

56
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:53,290
Press Shift and L to activate the Polygonal Lasso tool, which lets you select jagged regions.

57
00:04:53,291 --> 00:04:57,571
Press Shift and L once more to activate the Magnetic Lasso tool.

58
00:04:59,530 --> 00:05:02,810
Next, M is the Marquee tool, the rectangular marquee.

59
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:05,971
As the name says, it selects in a rectangular shape.

60
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:10,570
Then when you press Control and T, the Free Transform tool activates.

61
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:15,240
If you right-click, you'll find all sorts of options.

62
00:05:15,241 --> 00:05:16,840
It's good to try them out one by one.

63
00:05:19,715 --> 00:05:20,670
Like this.

64
00:05:20,671 --> 00:05:23,390
You can adjust it this way and that.

65
00:05:23,415 --> 00:05:27,870
In particular, there's a feature called Warp that I use a lot.

66
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:33,950
You can flip it horizontally.

67
00:05:33,951 --> 00:05:35,390
You can also flip it vertically.

68
00:05:39,555 --> 00:05:40,750
And it can rotate too.

69
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:46,510
If you hold R and drag, the screen rotates.

70
00:05:47,765 --> 00:05:51,310
Press the Escape key to return to normal.

71
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,910
If you hold the spacebar and drag, the screen moves.

72
00:05:56,570 --> 00:05:59,880
The slash key locks the layer.

73
00:05:59,881 --> 00:06:03,880
See how the padlock icon toggles on and off here?

74
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:09,400
When this icon is active, you can only paint within that layer.

75
00:06:09,401 --> 00:06:11,480
It's similar to the mask feature, right?

76
00:06:11,481 --> 00:06:12,920
It's something I use often.

77
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,820
Control and J duplicates the layer.

78
00:06:17,821 --> 00:06:18,940
See, it's been duplicated.

79
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:23,800
Control and E is the key to merge layers.

80
00:06:24,250 --> 00:06:29,160
Select two or more layers and press Control and E and they merge into one.

81
00:06:31,185 --> 00:06:34,970
There's also a feature to select just the objects inside a layer.

82
00:06:34,971 --> 00:06:39,770
Hold Control and click the layer thumbnail in the layers panel.

83
00:06:39,771 --> 00:06:42,771
Then only the objects in that layer get selected.

84
00:06:43,505 --> 00:06:45,690
I use this a lot too.

85
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:54,620
After making a selection, press Control and J to copy just the selected area into a new layer.

86
00:06:56,365 --> 00:07:00,770
After selecting, press Control, Shift, and J to cut and paste.

87
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:03,900
It disappears from the lower layer, see.

88
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:06,460
It's moved to a new layer.

89
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:17,040
If you want to copy multiple layers within a selection at once, press Control, Shift, and C, and only the selected region is copied.

90
00:07:18,330 --> 00:07:23,660
When you paste, you can see the entire selected area copied, regardless of the layers.

91
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:30,120
Since I use layer functions flexibly and often, it's good to learn them.

92
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:34,860
F5 brings up the brush settings panel.

93
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:41,381
If it's grayed out, select the brush or eraser tool and it becomes active.

94
00:07:42,030 --> 00:07:44,910
Control and S is the save shortcut.

95
00:07:44,911 --> 00:07:50,430
It saves the current work to the current file name, overwriting it.

96
00:07:50,615 --> 00:07:54,520
If you press Alt, Control, and S, you can save in many different formats.

97
00:07:59,665 --> 00:08:00,730
This is the Mixer Brush tool.

98
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:03,370
I use it a lot when blending colors.

99
00:08:03,371 --> 00:08:07,091
Originally C was the crop shortcut, but I reassigned it myself.

100
00:08:07,505 --> 00:08:12,291
To change it, go to Edit, then Keyboard Shortcuts.

101
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:14,460
Then click on the tool.

102
00:08:15,745 --> 00:08:16,940
Scroll down and you'll see the Mixer Brush tool.

103
00:08:18,835 --> 00:08:26,220
Click it, type C, and press Accept.

104
00:08:28,375 --> 00:08:33,280
And since C was originally assigned to crop, scroll back up.

105
00:08:35,955 --> 00:08:40,021
Delete all the shortcut keys for the crop-related functions.

106
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:46,840
The flip-horizontal shortcut is really important.

107
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:52,000
Even though it's a very important key, it has no default shortcut.

108
00:08:52,001 --> 00:08:55,440
That's why I assigned it to Control and G.

109
00:08:55,441 --> 00:08:56,720
The way to do it is: Edit, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Application Menus.

110
00:08:57,985 --> 00:09:10,640
Click on the Image menu and scroll down, and there's Flip Canvas Horizontal.

111
00:09:11,205 --> 00:09:16,880
Click it, type Control and G, and press Accept.

112
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,920
The flip-horizontal function is one I use all the time.

113
00:09:21,921 --> 00:09:25,760
They say the two hemispheres of the brain are used differently.

114
00:09:25,761 --> 00:09:33,040
Because you focus on one side, the picture can look off when flipped, and this lets you get rid of that effect.

115
00:09:33,041 --> 00:09:38,160
By flipping a picture you've grown used to, it lets you see it from a fresh perspective.

116
00:09:38,161 --> 00:09:40,960
I use very few shortcuts, personally.

117
00:09:40,961 --> 00:09:44,560
Maybe because I hated studying so much, I really dislike memorizing things.

118
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:47,760
I didn't memorize the other features because I hardly use them.

119
00:09:48,209 --> 00:09:54,201
To put it nicely, the shortcuts I use are like my compact, real-world shortcut set.

120
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:00,711
There are a couple of things to set up in Preferences.

121
00:10:01,155 --> 00:10:04,030
Go to the File Handling tab in Preferences.

122
00:10:05,370 --> 00:10:11,500
Check Automatically Save Recovery Information Every and set the interval.

123
00:10:11,505 --> 00:10:13,580
I have mine set to ten minutes.

124
00:10:13,581 --> 00:10:23,900
This way, if Photoshop suddenly crashes or your computer turns off, when you relaunch Photoshop it loads the most recent recovery file saved every ten minutes.

125
00:10:23,901 --> 00:10:24,901
It's like insurance.

126
00:10:25,505 --> 00:10:27,180
Everyone should have a little insurance, right?

127
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:37,651
Then go to the Performance tab and set the History States to anywhere from 100 to 300, depending on your computer's performance.

128
00:10:39,150 --> 00:10:42,110
That increases the amount of work history that gets stored.

129
00:10:42,270 --> 00:10:47,470
So if you make a mistake or don't like the picture, you can go further back in time, right?

130
00:10:47,471 --> 00:10:49,150
Next is the Scratch Disks.

131
00:10:50,415 --> 00:10:56,360
SSDs or HDDs help play a role similar to flash memory.

132
00:10:56,361 --> 00:11:00,001
I check all the storage devices I own.

133
00:11:05,235 --> 00:11:09,580
The first is the brush I mainly use for line drawing.

134
00:11:09,581 --> 00:11:12,780
I'll draw a pear as an example.

135
00:11:12,781 --> 00:11:14,620
It's a brush with a pencil feel.

136
00:11:14,621 --> 00:11:16,460
I also use it when coloring.

137
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:20,741
This picture was drawn mainly with this brush.

138
00:11:43,930 --> 00:11:47,610
The next brush is a round brush with a texture applied to it.

139
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:49,450
I use it for broad strokes.

140
00:12:08,310 --> 00:12:12,920
The third brush is the same as the previous one, but with a smaller texture size.

141
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:52,080
The fourth brush is the third brush with only the color jitter removed.

142
00:12:52,081 --> 00:12:54,080
Can you see the difference?

143
00:12:54,160 --> 00:12:55,680
Honestly, there's not much difference.

144
00:12:55,681 --> 00:13:00,320
These second, third, and fourth brushes are like brothers.

145
00:13:00,321 --> 00:13:03,401
I use them interchangeably for similar purposes.

146
00:13:22,255 --> 00:13:25,841
The fifth brush is just a brush, nothing special.

147
00:13:28,380 --> 00:13:32,151
The sixth brush is one I used a lot in the past.

148
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:36,990
This picture was drawn mainly with this brush.

149
00:13:38,110 --> 00:13:40,110
You can see the brush grain, right?

150
00:13:40,111 --> 00:13:45,150
Since it's a brush with a fixed grain direction, I use it while rotating it like this.

151
00:13:46,865 --> 00:13:50,330
Used this way, it gives a charcoal drawing feel.

152
00:13:50,331 --> 00:13:53,971
It's not as much as before, but I still use it sometimes.

153
00:14:06,945 --> 00:14:09,260
Next is the airbrush.

154
00:14:09,261 --> 00:14:13,180
Since it's such a well-known and much-used brush, I'll skip explaining it.

155
00:14:28,190 --> 00:14:31,911
The eighth brush is a relatively soft brush.

156
00:14:33,075 --> 00:14:35,020
I use it as a stand-in for the airbrush.

157
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:39,860
It has a slight texture so it's not boring.

158
00:14:42,650 --> 00:14:46,341
The ninth brush is one I sometimes use when coloring.

159
00:14:46,810 --> 00:14:52,501
I mainly use it as a Mixer Brush, and it has a nice soft spreading feel.

160
00:15:21,210 --> 00:15:26,170
The tenth brush is a very rough-feeling brush.

161
00:15:26,171 --> 00:15:30,970
I don't use it much; it's best for local areas.

162
00:15:30,971 --> 00:15:34,170
If you use this kind of brush a lot, your picture gets messy.

163
00:16:01,375 --> 00:16:04,691
The eleventh brush also has a textured feel.

164
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:06,330
I use it very rarely.

165
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:46,540
The twelfth brush is one I briefly used for sketching in the past.

166
00:16:46,541 --> 00:16:49,861
Now that I use the first brush, I don't use it much.

167
00:16:54,815 --> 00:17:00,380
The thirteenth brush is a type that blends color well, so it's good for coloring.

168
00:17:00,381 --> 00:17:04,380
It has texture, but among textured brushes it's relatively soft.

169
00:17:34,244 --> 00:17:38,080
The fourteenth brush is an airbrush with texture applied.

170
00:17:38,694 --> 00:17:40,320
I use this one very rarely too.

171
00:17:55,419 --> 00:17:58,300
Next is an explanation of the Mixer Brush tool.

172
00:17:58,380 --> 00:18:01,340
I changed its shortcut to C.

173
00:18:01,341 --> 00:18:05,100
The tool is different, but the brush is the same.

174
00:18:05,101 --> 00:18:08,060
I mainly use this ninth brush.

175
00:18:08,064 --> 00:18:13,260
It blends the color picked by the eyedropper with the color on the canvas.

176
00:18:13,261 --> 00:18:17,660
Depending on the brush, it blends strongly or weakly.

177
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:19,660
It's good to try it in various ways.

178
00:18:21,259 --> 00:18:25,500
Let me continue the apple a bit more using various brushes.

179
00:18:25,660 --> 00:18:32,780
About seventy percent of my use is the brushes in the first row, and about thirty percent is the second row.

180
00:18:32,781 --> 00:18:35,740
I hardly use the third row.

181
00:18:35,741 --> 00:18:40,860
You might covet the brushes I use, but finding the brushes that suit you comes first.

182
00:18:42,229 --> 00:18:43,800
Honestly, brushes are just a helper.

183
00:18:44,839 --> 00:18:48,081
You should be able to draw a picture with the default brushes alone.

184
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:56,170
But if using various brushes has helped you find the joy of drawing, then I'm fine with that.

185
00:19:00,464 --> 00:19:02,970
What's your working environment like?

186
00:19:02,970 --> 00:19:08,490
I'm currently a freelancer, so I work at home, and I have a separate work room.

187
00:19:08,491 --> 00:19:12,570
I really dislike someone talking next to me or watching me when I work.

188
00:19:13,159 --> 00:19:16,490
I'm used to working in a secluded space.

189
00:19:16,491 --> 00:19:21,930
If you prefer working in a secluded space like me, you'll run into issues when you work at a company.

190
00:19:21,931 --> 00:19:28,890
At the company, I sat in a corner seat and wallpapered my desk with IKEA wood grain to keep myself separated from the surroundings.

191
00:19:29,665 --> 00:19:31,980
That was only possible thanks to my boss's consideration.

192
00:19:32,544 --> 00:19:35,100
Now I can do it because I'm a freelancer.

193
00:19:35,101 --> 00:19:38,901
I was lucky, but I can't say I overcame the need for isolation.

194
00:19:38,939 --> 00:19:45,020
If you're thinking of getting hired, you need to get used to working in shared spaces.

195
00:19:45,021 --> 00:19:53,580
It'd be good to go to a studio where you can work with other people, or try live streaming on Discord or YouTube.

196
00:19:53,581 --> 00:19:56,981
When I work, I sometimes watch videos or listen to music.

197
00:19:57,689 --> 00:20:01,690
When the early stages require creative thinking, I don't watch or listen.

198
00:20:01,691 --> 00:20:07,731
During the later rendering work, which has become repetitive labor by now, I watch or listen to stave off boredom.

199
00:20:09,179 --> 00:20:13,950
Without coffee I feel anxious, and I always keep snacks stocked.

200
00:20:13,951 --> 00:20:17,390
These days I sometimes work with my puppy beside me.

201
00:20:17,390 --> 00:20:22,110
I think good drawing comes from a psychologically safe working environment.

202
00:20:22,909 --> 00:20:26,830
Within the given situation, try to build the working environment that suits you best.

203
00:20:29,110 --> 00:20:34,520
This time, we looked at setting up your working environment and how to manage brushes.

204
00:20:34,521 --> 00:20:39,480
Next chapter, we'll look at trendy and efficient figure drawing.
